 "title": "100: Sandboxing & Escape",
 "desc": "Goal: Understand seccomp-BPF syscall filtering and sandbox escape techniques. Linux seccomp-BPF attaches a BPF (Berkeley Packet Filter) program that inspects every syscall before execution. The filter examines the syscall number and arguments, returning an action: SECCOMP_RET_ALLOW, SECCOMP_RET_KILL, SECCOMP_RET_ERRNO, or SECCOMP_RET_TRACE. A typical sandbox blocks dangerous syscalls like execve, fork, and ptrace while allowing read, write, mmap, and exit. Escape strategies include: using allowed syscalls creatively (e.g., mprotect to make pages executable, then jump to shellcode), exploiting architecture confusion between x86 int 0x80 and x86-64 syscall (different syscall number tables), and TOCTOU races on syscall arguments in shared memory.",
 "mode": "asm-quiz",
 "arch": "x86",
 "source": [
  "// seccomp-BPF sandbox setup",
  "#include <seccomp.h>",
  "",
  "void setup_sandbox() {",
  "    scmp_filter_ctx ctx;",
  "    ctx = seccomp_init(SCMP_ACT_KILL);",
  "    // Default: KILL any syscall not allowed",
  "    seccomp_rule_add(ctx, SCMP_ACT_ALLOW,",
  "        SCMP_SYS(read), 0);",
  "    seccomp_rule_add(ctx, SCMP_ACT_ALLOW,",
  "        SCMP_SYS(write), 0);",
  "    seccomp_rule_add(ctx, SCMP_ACT_ALLOW,",
  "        SCMP_SYS(exit), 0);",
  "    // execve, fork, ptrace = BLOCKED",
  "    seccomp_load(ctx);",
  "}",
  "",
  "// Bypass: use allowed write() to exfil data",
  "// Or: mprotect (if allowed) + shellcode",
  "// Or: x32 ABI confusion (bit 30 trick)"
 ],
